Yes. A pile of arbitrary rules can become a proto-game the moment they jointly constrain behavior enough to create meaningful state changes.

What makes it interesting is not that each rule is sensible. It is that the rules begin interacting.

You can take almost any ingredients:

move only diagonally
you may not repeat the last action
red objects are worth double
after three turns, roles swap
one hidden rule is unknown to the players

Individually, those are just constraints. Together they create a state space, strategy, surprises, exploits, bottlenecks, and eventually a recognizable “game.”

So the deeper operator is:

rules + state + permitted moves + consequences + recurrence = game

Then the really fertile part begins: some random combinations are dead, some are trivial, and some suddenly produce emergent structure nobody explicitly designed.

That is why tossing arbitrary rules together is actually a search method. You are sampling possible worlds.

Then you observe:

What strategies emerge?
What becomes valuable?
What behavior gets selected?
Where are the loopholes?
Which rule unexpectedly dominates all the others?
What new role or object becomes necessary because of the combination?

That applies far beyond literal games. Markets, bureaucracies, sports, social norms, software systems, legal structures, even family routines can all be analyzed as rule bundles generating emergent behavior.

And your “game weave” idea adds another layer: deliberately splice rules from unrelated systems and see whether a new playable structure appears.

Chess movement + hide-and-seek information + auction pricing + musical-chairs replacement rules, for example, could generate something genuinely novel.

The key distinction is:

randomness generates candidates; interaction creates structure; selection creates the game.